 `build/boot/diag/AsdeStageProbe.codex` as a stage: finding 4, the last of
 Track B's metal questions and the riskiest thing in the ladder, so it runs
 LAST.

 82583V CTRL bit 5: "When set to 1b, the MAC ignores the speed indicated by
 the PHY ... This bit must be set to 0b in the 82583V." Whether the part on
 the desk behaves that way is the question; three flights have failed to
 answer it and each failure decided a piece of this ordering.

 WHY THE ARMS RUN BEFORE THE RESET. The 2026-08-11 flight died INSIDE
 `e1000-reset`, upstream of both arms the image existed to compare, so ASDE
 came back untested rather than disproved. Firmware hands this part over with
 CTRL.SLU already set (CTRL=0x180240 on that flight, SLU is bit 6), and
 `na-bring-up-after` is the whole of the bring-up except the reset, so both
 arms can be compared without pulsing RST at all. They run first. The reset
 rides last as ONE row, the control against 08-13: the five-way split that
 named which MMIO operation wedged has been answered twice, a warm reset
 completes on this part, and the staged version is in the depot history if a
 wedge ever comes back.

 WHY ASDE=1 RUNS FIRST. The 2026-08-13 flight returned identical values from
 both arms and could not say why, because three explanations survived it:
 ASDE does nothing here, the ASDE write does not stick, or the second arm
 inherited a link the first had already established. Running ASDE=1 first,
 from the state firmware left, kills the third; reading CTRL back after each
 arm kills the second.

 WHY A ROW GOES DOWN BEFORE THE TOUCH THAT FOLLOWS IT, and this is the whole
 instrument rather than an ordering preference. The 2026-08-11 stick came
 back with NOTHING written, and two boots have established that a wedge
 leaves the glass intact (HardwareSitting 2026-08-05: rows painted, machine
 stalled, rows still readable). On that box the glass is the only bank that
 has ever worked, so the last line standing in this stage's slot names the
 last thing that completed and the first thing that did not.

 WHAT THE LIFT COST, said plainly because it is a real loss. The probe owned
 the whole screen and painted about a dozen rows; a ladder stage owns a slot
 a few lines tall. The progressive lines roll within that slot and the slot
 is cleared when they wrap, so the glass carries the LAST few rather than all
 of them. Everything is in the bank as well, for the boxes where a bank
 works. Last-step-standing survives; the full transcript on glass does not.

 THE RUN ORDER IS NOT THE STATE PRECEDENCE and the two must not be confused.
 The SLU discriminator runs AFTER the arms, because the run order is decided
 by wedge risk. Its answer DOMINATES them: if CTRL is read-only to us then
 nothing we wrote was written and both arms are describing a register we
 never changed.

 We say:

Section: States

  dasd-states : List Text = ["differs", "same", "asde-stuck", "ctrl-ro", "no-link", "no-part", "rejected", "bar-bad"]

Section: The Slot

 Rolling within the stage's own slot: each line takes the next row, and the
 slot is cleared when the count wraps, so the newest line is never painted
 over an older one that is still readable beside it. Serial carries the same
 line for the bed, where a transcript is free and a photograph is not.

  dasd-slot-rows : DiagCtx -> Integer
  dasd-slot-rows (c) = (c.dc-slot-h) / (16 * (c.dc-scale))

  dasd-rows : DiagCtx -> Integer
  dasd-rows (c) =
   let h = dasd-slot-rows c
   in if h < 1 then 1 else h

 TWO THINGS THIS GOT WRONG ON THE FIRST FLIGHT and both showed on the glass
 at the ASUS as an orange box full of noise (red, 2026-08-20).

 IT CLEARS THE ROW IT IS ABOUT TO WRITE. Clearing only when the count wrapped
 was not enough: gop-draw-text paints glyph pixels and not a background, so
 a shorter line drawn over a longer one left the older line's tail standing
 beside it and the slot filled with overlapping glyphs. The ladder never hits
 this because dg-text is always preceded by dg-clear-rows.

 IT FITS THE LINE TO THE SLOT. A picture stage's slot is the right-hand third
 (dg-text-end returns dc-w * 2 / 3 - 8), so at scale 2 on a 1920-wide
 panel it is about forty columns, and these readings are sixty to seventy
 characters. Painted unfitted they ran off the slot and off the panel. This is
 dg-fit's job in the ladder and the stage needs its own, because dg-fit
 lives in Diag.codex and measures against the row rather than the slot.

  dasd-cols : DiagCtx -> Integer
  dasd-cols (c) = ((c.dc-slot-w) - 8) / (8 * (c.dc-scale))

  dasd-fit : DiagCtx, Text -> Text
  dasd-fit (c) (s) =
   let n = dasd-cols c
   in if n < 4 then ""
   else if text-length s <= n then s
   else substring s 0 (n - 3) & "..."

 ROW 0 IS THE STEP AND NOTHING ELSE EVER PAINTS THERE. Rolling every line
 through every row made the three lines standing appear OUT OF ORDER -- the
 bed photograph after the first fix showed row 2 holding an older line than
 rows 0 and 1 -- so a reader at the board cannot tell which of them is the
 last. That defeats the one property this slot exists for. A step line now
 clears the whole slot and takes row 0, and readings roll through the rows
 beneath it, so the top line always names the operation IN PROGRESS and the
 lines under it are its readings.

  dasd-at : DiagCtx, Integer, Text, Integer -> Integer
  dasd-at (c) (row) (s) (col) =
   let y = (c.dc-slot-y) + row * 16 * (c.dc-scale)
   in let cleared = gop-fill-rect (c.dc-base) (c.dc-stride) (c.dc-slot-x) y (c.dc-slot-w) (16 * (c.dc-scale)) diag-col-bg
   in gop-draw-text (c.dc-base) (c.dc-stride) (c.dc-font) (c.dc-slot-x) y (dasd-fit c s) col (c.dc-scale) 0

  dasd-paint : DiagCtx, Integer, Text, Integer -> Integer
  dasd-paint (c) (n) (s) (col) =
   let rows = dasd-rows c
   in let below = if rows > 1 then rows - 1 else 1
   in dasd-at c (if rows > 1 then 1 + int-mod n below else 0) s col

  dasd-open : DiagCtx, Text -> Integer
  dasd-open (c) (s) =
   let wiped = gop-fill-rect (c.dc-base) (c.dc-stride) (c.dc-slot-x) (c.dc-slot-y) (c.dc-slot-w) (c.dc-slot-h) diag-col-bg
   in dasd-at c 0 s diag-col-warn

 A READING GOES TO THE GLASS ONLY. The ladder already prints every bank line
 to serial and banks it, so printing it here again puts it on the wire twice
 and `Compare-Rows` in `diag-arm.ps1` counts the difference as serial and
 file disagreeing -- which is what it is for.

 A STEP LINE GOES TO BOTH, and it uses the ladder's live-progress convention
 (`<stage> entering <step>`) because it is the same thing nicinit's lines
 are: serial-only by design, skipped by the compare, and unbankable because
 the step it names may not return. That is exactly why it exists.

  dasd-step : DiagCtx, Integer, Text -> [Console] Integer
  dasd-step (c) (n) (s) = act
   p <- print-line-uni ("asde entering " & s)
   dasd-open c ("-> " & s)
  end

Section: The SLU Discriminator

 Every CTRL bit this probe has ever written is one that cannot report whether
 the write took: SLU was already set by firmware, ASDE does not stick, and
 RST self-clears, so "never written" and "written and completed" both read as
 settled. So clear a bit that IS currently set and read it back. SLU is the
 only one available. If SLU still reads set immediately after we cleared it,
 CTRL is read-only to us and no link behaviour is needed to know it -- which
 matters well beyond this stage, because RX and TX bring-up write RCTL, TCTL
 and the ring registers, and a silent CSR write path would surface there as a
 receive path that never receives.

  dasd-clear-slu : Integer = #FFFFFFFF - e1000-ctrl-slu

  dasd-slu-line : Text, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dasd-slu-line (label) (ctrl) (status) =
   label & " CTRL=" & show ctrl & " SLUbit=" & na-yn (bit-and ctrl e1000-ctrl-slu)
     & " STATUS=" & show status & " LU=" & na-yn (bit-and status na-lu)

  DasdSlu = record {
    ds-writable : Boolean,
    ds-lines : List Text
  }

  dasd-slu : DiagCtx, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Mmio, Console] DasdSlu
  dasd-slu (c) (mmio) (n) = act
   e0 <- dasd-step c n "slu s1 read CTRL and STATUS"
   let c0 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
   in let s0 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-status
   in let l1 = dasd-slu-line "slu s1 before" c0 s0
   in let p1 = dasd-paint c (n + 1) l1 diag-col-dim
   in act
    e1 <- dasd-step c (n + 2) "slu s2 CLEAR SLU and read back"
    let wclr = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-ctrl (bit-and c0 dasd-clear-slu)
    in let c1 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
    in let s1 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-status
    in let took = bit-and c1 e1000-ctrl-slu == 0
    in let l2 = dasd-slu-line "slu s2 cleared" c1 s1
    in let p2 = dasd-paint c (n + 3) l2 (if took then diag-col-note else diag-col-bad)
    in act
     e2 <- dasd-step c (n + 4) "slu s3 settle 10ms"
     let st = e1000-settle-mdio mmio
     in let c2 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
     in let s2 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-status
     in let l3 = dasd-slu-line "slu s3 after 10ms" c2 s2
     in let p3 = dasd-paint c (n + 5) l3 diag-col-note
     in act
      e3 <- dasd-step c (n + 6) "slu s4 RESTORE CTRL"
      let wr = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-ctrl c0
      in let st2 = e1000-settle-mdio mmio
      in let c3 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
      in let l4 = dasd-slu-line "slu s4 restored" c3 (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-status)
      in let p4 = dasd-paint c (n + 7) l4 diag-col-good
      in DasdSlu { ds-writable = took, ds-lines = [l1, l2, l3, l4] }
     end
    end
   end
  end

Section: The Arms

  dasd-arm-line : Text, NicAsdeArm, Integer -> Text
  dasd-arm-line (label) (arm) (ctrl) =
   na-line label arm & " CTRLback=" & show ctrl & " ASDEbit=" & na-yn (bit-and ctrl e1000-ctrl-asde)

  dasd-arms-differ : NicAsdeArm, NicAsdeArm -> Boolean
  dasd-arms-differ (a) (b) =
   (a.na-status) /= (b.na-status) | (a.na-aneg) /= (b.na-aneg)
     | (a.na-gave) /= (b.na-gave) | (a.na-phy) /= (b.na-phy)

  dasd-answer : NicAsdeArm, NicAsdeArm, Integer, Boolean -> Text
  dasd-answer (on) (off) (onctrl) (writable) =
   "w=" & na-yn (if writable then 1 else 0)
     & " stick=" & na-yn (bit-and onctrl e1000-ctrl-asde)
     & " diff=" & na-yn (if dasd-arms-differ on off then 1 else 0)
     & " on=" & show (on.na-status) & " off=" & show (off.na-status)
     & " g1=" & na-gave-text (on.na-gave) & " g0=" & na-gave-text (off.na-gave)

Section: The Stage

 The reset is the control against 08-13 and it is the last thing that runs,
 so its row is painted before it is attempted and nothing is read from it
 that any state word depends on.

 IT QUIESCES FIRST, and that is a change of 2026-08-20 made after this stage
 WEDGED THE ASUS on its first flight, sitting on the RESET step for five
 minutes.

 THE EVIDENCE IS THE BOUND, not the story. `e1000-await-reset` is a COUNT of
 a million MMIO reads, about a second on metal, so `e1000-reset` MUST return
 within seconds -- 0 if the bit never clears, 1 if it does. Five minutes is
 therefore not a slow reset. It is the reads themselves no longer returning,
 which is a claim about the part having stopped answering MMIO at all.

 THE EXPLANATION, and it is an explanation rather than a measurement:
 `e1000-reset` does not quiesce. It masks IMC, pulses RST, waits, masks again,
 and never writes RCTL or TCTL. On the standalone probe that was a reset of a
 part firmware had just handed over. In the ladder, nicinit and b3 have each
 run `e1000-init` and nicring has built live rings by the time this runs, so
 it was pulsing RST on a receiver that was RUNNING with descriptors in flight.

 THE CHANGE DOES NOT DEPEND ON THAT EXPLANATION BEING RIGHT (red's ruling).
 Quiescing a DMA-capable part before pulsing reset is correct either way:
 nicring already does it, and `E1000e`'s own prose says disabling the receiver
 IS the quiesce. The hazard is recorded at `e1000-reset` itself, not only
 here, because the convention is caller-responsible and every future caller
 can make the same mistake.

 IT IS TWO STEPS NOW, NOT ONE, and that is not the five-way split coming
 back: it separates the operation being ADDED from the one that wedged, so a
 box that stops inside the quiesce is told apart from one that stops inside
 the reset. The five-way split stays retired -- it would only name which MMIO
 stopped answering, which is what we already know.

 AND A COMPLETED RESET ON THE NEXT FLIGHT IS NOT PROOF. If the next stick
 gets past this row it is CONSISTENT with the explanation above and nothing
 more; other things will have changed by then.

  dasd-reset : DiagCtx, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Mmio, Console] Text
  dasd-reset (c) (mmio) (n) = act
   q <- dasd-step c n "RESET s1 quiesce, RCTL=0 TCTL=0, before pulsing RST"
   let quiet = e1000-quiesce mmio
   in act
    p <- dasd-step c (n + 1) "RESET s2 warm reset, the control for 08-13"
    let settled = e1000-reset mmio
    in let t = na-touch mmio
    in let l = "reset done settled=" & show settled & " CTRL=" & show (t.nt-ctrl) & " STATUS=" & show (t.nt-status)
    in let p2 = dasd-paint c (n + 2) l diag-col-good
    in l
   end
  end

 EVERY LINE THAT NAMES A STEP IS PAINTED BEFORE THAT STEP RUNS, and the
 result line after it. Painted after, a wedge leaves the PREVIOUS step's name
 standing and the glass lies about which operation killed the box -- the same
 off-by-one as reading a row that has not run yet, and on this box the glass
 is the whole record. The `->` lines are the ones that carry that property;
 the lines without it are readings of something that already returned.

  dasd-run-part : DiagCtx, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio, Console] DiagResult
  dasd-run-part (c) (mmio) (dev-id) = act
   let p0 = dasd-paint c 0 ("eligible mmio=" & show mmio) diag-col-note
   in act
    e0 <- dasd-step c 1 "touch, read-only"
    let touch = na-touch mmio
    in let ltouch = na-touch-line mmio touch
    in let p1 = dasd-paint c 2 ltouch diag-col-note
    in act
     e1 <- dasd-step c 3 "arm ASDE=1 FIRST, no reset, from the firmware state"
     let on = na-bring-up-after mmio dev-id True
     in let onctrl = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
     in let lon = dasd-arm-line "asde=1 first" on onctrl
     in let p3 = dasd-paint c 4 lon diag-col-good
     in act
      e2 <- dasd-step c 5 "arm ASDE=0 SECOND, no reset"
      let off = na-bring-up-after mmio dev-id False
      in let offctrl = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
      in let loff = dasd-arm-line "asde=0 second" off offctrl
      in let p4 = dasd-paint c 6 loff diag-col-good
      in act
       slu <- dasd-slu c mmio 7
       rst <- dasd-reset c mmio 15
       let stuck = bit-and onctrl e1000-ctrl-asde == 0
       in let differ = dasd-arms-differ on off
       in let nolink = (on.na-gave) == na-gave-nolink & (off.na-gave) == na-gave-nolink
       in let state = if (slu.ds-writable) == False then "ctrl-ro"
             else if stuck then "asde-stuck"
             else if nolink then "no-link"
             else if differ then "differs"
             else "same"
       in let colour = if state == "differs" then diag-col-good
             else if state == "same" then diag-col-note
             else diag-col-bad
       in let answer = dasd-answer on off onctrl (slu.ds-writable)
       in diag-result state colour [answer, lon, loff] ([answer, ltouch, lon, loff] & (slu.ds-lines) & [rst])
      end
     end
    end
   end
  end

 The bus is scanned rather than taken from the ladder's context on purpose:
 the probe's scope was bus 0 and every flight reading is against that scope,
 so widening it here would silently change what a repeat flight compares to.

  dasd-run : DiagCtx -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio, Console] DiagResult
  dasd-run (c) = act
   let scan = pci-scan-bus 0
   in when e1000-find scan
    is None -> diag-result "no-part" diag-col-dim ["no Intel NIC on bus 0 (scope: bus 0 only)"] ["no Intel NIC on bus 0 (scope: bus 0 only)"]
    is Just (dev) ->
      if na-eligible dev == False then diag-result "rejected" diag-col-warn [na-reject-line dev] [na-reject-line dev]
      else if e1000-bar-verdict (na-bar-of dev) /= e1000-bar-ok
        then diag-result "bar-bad" diag-col-bad ["verdict=" & na-verdict-text (e1000-bar-verdict (na-bar-of dev)) & ", the part was not touched"] ["verdict=" & na-verdict-text (e1000-bar-verdict (na-bar-of dev)) & ", the part was not touched"]
      else dasd-run-part c (na-mmio-of dev) (dev.pci-device-id)
  end

